// `?` on a plain value. It needs something that is either a success or a failure, and an Int
// is neither — so the refusal names the type it was handed rather than talking about generics.
//
// One of four fail fixtures added with `?` in v0.0.221. They exist because the operator had NO
// implementation on the Burxt side while the suite reported 142 of 142 pass programs, and the
// reason nothing caught it is that **a pass fixture cannot tell "supported" from "not examined."**
function f() -> Int {
    let n: Int = 5;
    let y: Int = n?;
    return y;
}
print(f());
